course: english
lesson: 4
title: Vowel teams, split digraphs and choosing the spelling
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A silent e at the end of a word reaches back over one consonant and makes the vowel say its name: hop becomes hope, kit becomes kite, cub becomes cube. Schools call a-e, i-e, o-e and u-e split digraphs, because the two-letter team is split by a consonant; Lucy's teacher draws a little bridge from the a to the e in «cake» so she stops reading «cack».
explain: English often has several spellings for one sound. Tom in Newcastle grumbles that «boat», «snow», «home» and «toe» all say /oa/ and are all spelled differently; he is right, and it is still learnable. Some choices follow where the sound sits: «ai» usually in the middle of a word (rain, train), «ay» at the end (play, day).
explain: It works the other way too: one spelling can make more than one sound. «ea» says /ee/ in «eat» but /e/ in «head»; «ow» says /ow/ in «cow» but /oa/ in «snow»; «ear» says /ear/ in «hear» but /air/ in «bear»; «oo» is long in «moon» and short in «book». A good reader tries one sound, and if it makes no real word that fits the sentence, tries the other.
explain: The letter y is a shape-shifter. At the start of «yes» it is a consonant; in «my» it says /ie/ and in «happy» it says /ee/, so there it works as a vowel. And «ir», «ur» and «er» all make the same sound (bird, burn, her), but each word has its usual spelling, so you cannot pick freely.
explain: Accents shape what we hear. In parts of the North of England some people say «book» with a long sound, nearly like «boohk»; the spelling stays the same everywhere. The trick is to learn words with the same sound in groups: an «igh» family (high, light, night), an «ew» family (new, flew, chew), a «ue» family (blue, glue, true).
fact: In a split digraph (a-e, e-e, i-e, o-e, u-e) the silent e makes the vowel say its name: hop/hope, kit/kite, cub/cube.
fact: «ai» usually comes in the middle of a word (rain) and «ay» at the end (play).
fact: The /oa/ sound has several spellings: oa (boat), ow (snow), o-e (home), oe (toe).
fact: «igh» is a three-letter team (a trigraph) that says /ie/: high, light, night, bright.
fact: «ea» has two common sounds: /ee/ in «eat» and /e/ in «head».
fact: «ow» can say /ow/ as in «cow» or /oa/ as in «snow»; the sentence tells you which.
fact: «oo» has a long sound in «moon» and a short sound in «book», and accents differ in how they say «book».
fact: «ew» (new) and «ue» (blue) can both say /oo/; which one a word uses is learned word by word.
fact: «ear» says /ear/ in «hear» and /air/ in «bear», «pear» and «wear».
fact: «y» is a consonant at the start of «yes» but works as a vowel in «my» (/ie/) and «happy» (/ee/).
fact: «ir», «ur» and «er» make the same sound (bird, burn, her), but each word has its own usual spelling.
fact: When a spelling has two possible sounds, try one, then the other, and keep the one that makes a real word that fits.
fact: Slang in this lesson: «howay» means come on and «canny» here means quite (North East England); «proper job» means well done and «my lover» is a friendly greeting (West Country); «heaps» means a lot (Australia); «can or not» means «is it allowed?» in Singapore English.
exercise: Lucy reads «cake» as «cack». What does the bridge from a to e show her? | The e makes the a say its name | The e is said loudly at the end | The a and e swap places | The k should be read twice
exercise: Which pair shows the silent e changing a word? | hop and hope | cat and cot | bed and bad | hat and hit, a vowel swap
exercise: Max asks why «rain» has ai but «play» has ay. What is the usual pattern? | ai in the middle, ay at the end | ai for short words, ay for long ones | ai for nouns, ay for verbs | ay in the middle, ai at the end
exercise: Tom says «boat», «snow», «home» and «toe» share one sound. Which sound? | /oa/ | /ow/ | /oo/ | /o/ as in «hot»
exercise: What does «howay» mean in Newcastle? | Come on | Go away | How are you | Which way
exercise: Sadie writes «nite». Which team does «night» use for /ie/? | igh | ie | i-e | y
exercise: Dev reads «head» as «heed». What should he try? | The other sound of ea, /e/ | Reading the word backwards | Skipping the word | Adding a silent e
exercise: Chloe's dad asks about «ow» in «cow» and «snow». How do you choose? | Use the sentence to see which word fits | Always say /ow/ like in «cow» | Always say /oa/ like in «snow» | Pick whichever one is quicker and easier to say aloud
exercise: Ella finds «oo» in «moon» and «book». What does she learn? | One spelling, two sounds | Two spellings, one sound | One of the words is spelled wrong | The oo in book is silent
exercise: Harry in Sydney asks if «ew» in «new» is the same as «ue» in «blue». What is the answer? | Yes, both can say /oo/ | No, ew says /ee/ | No, ue is always silent | Yes, and you can swap them in any word
exercise: Ava reads «bear» as «beer». What sound does «ear» make in «bear»? | /air/ | /ear/ | /er/ | /ar/
exercise: Jo says y can never be a vowel. In which word does y work as a vowel? | happy | yes | yellow | yard
exercise: Zoe asks if she can choose any of «ir», «ur», «er». Can she? | No, each word has its usual spelling | Yes, any of them is fine anywhere | Yes, but only in Singapore English | No, only «er» is ever correct
exercise: What does «can or not» mean in Singapore English? | Is it allowed? | Can you open the can? | You can't do that | Can you hear me?
exercise: Which word has a split digraph? | kite | kit | knit | kitten
exercise: What does «heaps» mean in Australia? | A lot | A little | Piles of rubbish | Hurry up
test: «proper job, my lover! now read: the ___ was on the farm, cow or snow?». Which fits and why? | Cow, because a cow fits a farm sentence | Snow, because ow always says /oa/ | Cow, because ow always says /ow/ | Both fit the farm, so either answer is right
test: «the e on the end of hope does nothing, it's just decoration innit». Put him right. | Without it the word is «hop», a different word | He's right, «hope» and «hop» sound the same | He's right, silent letters never change anything | The e is said quietly, like «hop-eh»
test: «howay man, oa, ow, o-e, oe, all for one sound, it's canny daft, nobody can learn that». What is the wise move? | Sort words with that sound into columns and learn the common one first | Give up, because the spellings can never be learned | Use «oa» for every word with that sound, and the teacher will have to accept it | Spell each word however it feels that day
test: «nite» is on a shop sign. Sadie's mum says «see, it's spelled nite». What is true? | Signs can use playful spellings; the real word is «night» | The sign is right and «night» is old-fashioned | Both spellings are fine in school work | «nite» is the British spelling, «night» the American
test: What would happen if Dev only ever tried the /ee/ sound for «ea»? | He would misread words like «head» and «bread» | He would read every ea word correctly | He would read faster, because there's only one sound | Nothing, because ea only has one sound
test: «y's a consonant, it's in the alphabet with the consonants, no way it's a vowel». What is the fair reply? | At the start of «yes» it is, but in «my» and «happy» it works as a vowel | She is right, y is only ever a consonant | Y is only ever a vowel, it just looks like a consonant | Y is neither, it is a silent letter in every word it appears in, even «yes»
test: In a cartoon a bear says «I'm a beer!» because «ear» always says /ear/. What should the bear learn? | «ear» says /air/ in «bear», «pear» and «wear» | «bear» should be spelled «bare» for the animal | «ear» always says /ear/, so the cartoon is right | «bear» and «beer» are the same word
test: A friend from Lancashire says «book» so it nearly rhymes with «moon». Is she saying it wrong? | No, accents differ; the spelling stays the same | Yes, there is only one correct way to say «book» in English | Yes, and she should spell it «buke» | No, and the word should be spelled «boon»
test: «can or not, teacher, I write bird as burd, same sound one». What is the answer? | Not allowed: «bird» uses «ir», and each word has its usual spelling | Can, because all three spellings are the same | Can, but only in Singapore English | Not allowed: «bird» is spelled «berd»
test: ★★★ Which word becomes a NEW real word when you add a silent e to the end? | pin | ship | fish | bend
test: ★★★ How many words in this list have the /oa/ sound: boat, cow, snow, home, shout? | 3 | 2 | 4 | 5
test: ★★★ In «the wind blew the bread off the table», one word has the vowel sound of «true» and one the vowel sound of «head». Which pair is it? | blew and bread | wind and blew | bread and table | the and blew
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of a final silent e in a split digraph? | It causes the preceding vowel to be pronounced as its name | It is pronounced as a separate syllable at the end of the word | It serves solely as decoration and has no effect | It indicates that the word is of foreign origin
exam: Ascertain the course of action appropriate to a reader who encounters a letter team possessing two possible sounds. | To attempt each sound in turn and retain that which yields a real word suited to the sentence | To pronounce the team invariably in its more common manner, irrespective of the meaning of the sentence in which it stands | To omit the word entirely and continue reading | To pronounce each letter of the team separately
exam: Identify the principal distinction between «ai» and «ay» as representations of the same sound. | «ai» generally occurs within a word, whereas «ay» generally occurs at its end | «ai» occurs exclusively in nouns, whereas «ay» occurs exclusively in verbs | «ay» is the American form and «ai» the British form of the same spelling | There is no distinction of any kind, the two being interchangeable in all words
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the letter y. | It functions as a consonant at the beginning of certain words and as a vowel elsewhere | It functions invariably as a consonant in all words of the language | It is never pronounced and serves merely to mark the end of a word | It functions as a vowel exclusively in words borrowed from Greek
